    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Many students in academic or career transitions struggle to make decisions shaping their vocational path. They often make choices that are opportunistic and based on limited preconceptions rather than real experiences. According to a McKinsey survey, many students are making career education choices blindly with no connection to reality (McKinsey Survey, Aug-Sept 2012). Too often the case, such choices lead them to feel stuck on a career path that they don’t find personally meaningful or relevant to their real life. \r\n\r\nThe responsibility for helping students discover and navigate career pathway options has been in the hands of guidance counselors. However, as noted by the American School Counselor Association, more than half of students today say no one in their school has been helpful in advising on these options. Moreover, the majority of existing tools and services focus on providing quick and superficial solutions, rather than equipping students with transferable skills that empower them to proactively shape and continuously adapt their career path based on experiences throughout their academic and professional journey. Many existing solutions also fail to reach students who have little access to resources or come from different backgrounds. \r\n\r\nOur project, PaperHat, empowers students from diverse backgrounds to take the driver’s seat of their career navigation. PaperHat is a web-based learning platform that helps students learn a proactive approach to craft a fulfilling career path: life experimenting. Life experimenting is when we do small activities that help us get a sense, feel and taste of a bigger life experience. They require little time and money, are easy to do, and give us actionable insights. PaperHat guides students through the process of life experimentation, provides tutorials for the different steps as well as supporting features like crowdsourced life experiments from a global community of students, resources that support taking action, and a chatbot companion that provides guidance and emotional support, step by step.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Ning De-Eknamkul and Cecilia WoerthMueller. (2018). PaperHat: Craft a Fulfilling Career Path with Real-Life Experiments. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: https://purl.stanford.edu/pc081bs3957",
